## Block C — AI contracts, intake, and provider access

## T9 — Usage accounting, admission, processing tickets, and idempotency contracts
Reasoning: Cost protection must hold under concurrency and replay before any provider call exists; this task owns what 
the database and ticket code can prove on their own (review P5). Dispatch-dependent evidence moves to T12.
Task: Server-only usage records per company per day (America/Sao_Paulo). One `service_role`-only admission RPC that, 
in a single transaction holding the company settings row lock: (1) verifies the caller's current membership and editor 
rights on the note (for replays too); (2) looks up an existing operation for the ticket's idempotency key — if found 
with the same binding, returns its status without new admission, regardless of later settings or source changes; if 
found with a different binding, rejects; (3) otherwise, with the note row locked, verifies that the bound settings 
revision is current and that the note's revision and transcript identity equal the ticket's (stale source → conflict, 
nothing reserved), reserves usage against the limit, records the operation with the provider/model/effort snapshot, 
and returns the matching key and the bound transcript text from the same snapshot. Settlement once, against the 
admission day, by a `service_role`-only RPC; it counts every dispatched operation, refunds only pre-processing 
rejections, and releases the reservation (not counted) for an operation that demonstrably never sent a request (size 
preflight refusal, cancellation before send, error before dispatch); settlement is idempotent. The idempotency key is 
generated by the server at ticket issuance and embedded in the signed ticket (a client cannot choose a key or reuse 
one with a new ticket); the key expires with its ticket, 24 h after issuance, checked before any lookup or admission. 
Idempotency keys scoped to user+company+operation and bound to note id, source revision, transcript identity, settings 
revision. Signed processing tickets (issue time, idempotency key, user, company, note, source revision, transcript 
identity, settings revision) with HMAC-SHA256 using the required server-only env var `MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET` 
(≥32 bytes, no fallback — AI start refuses with a configuration error when missing), plus optional 
`MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET_PREVIOUS` accepted for verification only so a rotation keeps outstanding tickets valid 
for their 24 h; the local value is provisioned in `.env.local` and `.env.example` documents it. A ticket-issue route 
(called when the notice is shown) authorizes current membership and editor rights before signing. Operation records 
without content kept 30 days (pg_cron cleanup); an operation without outcome 10 minutes after dispatch becomes 
"failed" and stays counted (pg_cron); deleting notes or users never changes usage; no client role can read-modify 
usage beyond reading its own company's count.
Done when: Admission, settlement, replay lookup, and ticket issuance satisfy FR-022/FR-023/FR-031 at the database and 
route boundary, independently of any provider.
Verify by: Integration tests: five concurrent admissions at 19/20 → exactly one reserved, four refused (AC-028); same 
key + binding returns the existing operation across midnight; different binding rejected; direct 
`anon`/`authenticated` calls to admission, settlement, and usage tables denied (AC-028); a settings save racing an 
admission yields either admission under the old revision with the old snapshot or refusal — never a mixed snapshot 
(AC-025 DB side); a transcript replaced after ticket issuance makes the new admission a conflict with nothing 
reserved; a released never-dispatched reservation leaves usage unchanged and a second settlement changes nothing; the 
stale-operation job turns an operation backdated >10 min into "failed", still counted; the cleanup job removes records 
older than 30 days; note deletion leaves usage unchanged (AC-034 DB side); limit-reached response data and reset at 
00:00 America/Sao_Paulo (AC-016 DB side); ticket-issue route denies a removed member and a non-editor and embeds a 
server-generated key; `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
Covers: FR-022, FR-023, FR-031, BR-008, AC-016, AC-025, AC-028, AC-034
Root: T9
Depends on: T8

## T10 — Transcript intake: upload, attach, validation, normalization
Reasoning: The transcript must be validated and stored before any AI step (owner r4: saved at upload). T17's 
`create_meeting_note` requires a space of the caller's company and does not take a transcript; transcript writes need 
their own revision-checked, versioned path under the note row lock.
Task: Routes for "New note from transcript" (creates the note with title = file name ≤200, date = today, transcript 
stored, version created) and "Attach transcript" (editors only; replaces the stored transcript and transcript identity 
immediately; summary untouched; revision bumped). Files must be .txt/.vtt/.srt, non-empty, ≤1 MB, valid UTF-8 text; 
otherwise rejected with a message and no change. A server normalizer removes VTT/SRT cue numbers, timestamps, and cue 
settings while keeping speaker names and text; it is the single source of the text sent to providers (its 
received-text evidence is T12's).
Done when: Valid transcripts create or update notes; invalid ones change nothing.
Verify by: Integration tests through the routes for AC-010 (upload creates note immediately; attach replaces, summary 
unchanged), AC-011 (each invalid file rejected, no note created or changed, usage unchanged), AC-024; 
`supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
Covers: FR-012, FR-013, FR-014, BR-010, AC-010, AC-011, AC-024
Root: T10
Depends on: T17, T3

## T16 — Local fake AI server harness
Reasoning: Every AI test needs a deterministic stand-in for four provider wire protocols; separating it from the 
adapters gives it its own checkpoint (review P11).
Task: A test-only fake AI server under the meeting-notes test tree that speaks the Anthropic Messages API and the 
OpenAI Chat Completions/Responses shapes used by OpenAI, Kimi, and GLM; records every received request (headers 
without keys, body); and scripts outcomes per test: success with a given draft, error after processing, timeout, 
refusal, malformed JSON, each listed pre-processing rejection, unlisted statuses, overload after streaming began, 
connection drop after acceptance, and pause/resume. The DB integration suite's setup and the E2E runner start and stop 
it on a fixed local port.
Done when: Both test runners start the fake server, it answers each protocol with the scripted outcome, records 
requests, and shuts down cleanly.
Verify by: The meeting-notes DB suite setup starts it and a setup self-check sends one request per protocol and reads 
back the recorded request; the E2E config starts and stops it (config inspected; executed at T14).
